Revision history for Linux-Event-HTTP

0.001  UNRELEASED
    - Rename the distribution and supported public namespace to Linux::Event::HTTP.
    - Adopt the Linux::Event ecosystem charter as the project policy.
    - Prioritize correctness, ease of correct use, API clarity, maintainability,
      bridgeability, and then good performance for protocol-layer work.
    - Move competitive and exploratory HTTP benchmarking out of this distribution;
      perl-Benchmark-Web is the benchmark repository for cross-server work.
    - Remove the in-distribution benchmark tree and benchmark execution from CI; CI
      now focuses on build, behavioral tests, threaded Perl, and distribution
      integrity.
    - Add Linux::Event::HTTP public facades while treating the previous
      Linux::Event::Net::HTTP packages as non-public implementation details during
      the namespace migration.
    - Reject the former benchmark-only on_request_final callback from the supported
      Linux::Event::HTTP Server and Connection APIs.
    - Remove on_request_final forwarding from the private Server acceptance adapter.
    - Remove the native default-response shortcut from ordinary Response->end so
      complete, streamed, and deferred responses use one response state machine.
    - Retire and delete the dedicated xsresponse1 extension; the temporary private
      Response1 compatibility shim now only forces fallback to ordinary Response
      serialization while the old Connection implementation is being removed.
    - Remove benchmark-specific native-response and final-response tests while
      retaining the general response/framing/connection behavioral coverage.
    - Keep HTTP as a protocol library rather than a web application framework.
    - Preserve Linux::Event ownership of transport, TLS, buffering, backpressure,
      deadlines, event dispatch, and protocol handoff primitives.
    - Establish HTTP Server, Connection, Request, and Response as the public object
      model.
    - Keep streaming request bodies and streaming/deferred responses on one coherent
      request/response lifecycle.
    - Keep HTTPS as HTTP over Linux::Event TLS transport rather than a separate object
      hierarchy.
    - Preserve validated HTTP Upgrade as the bridge to separate protocol
      distributions such as Linux::Event::WebSocket.
    - Continue using picohttpparser internally for the current HTTP/1 request-head
      implementation while treating parser choice as replaceable implementation
      detail rather than public API.
    - Retain strict HTTP/1 framing validation for Host, Content-Length,
      Transfer-Encoding, persistence, and ambiguous request framing.
    - Retain chunked request decoding, response framing, Expect: 100-continue,
      keep-alive, pipelining, and TLS integration correctness coverage.
